Common C Pitfall #1: Uninitialized Variables
// BAD: Uninitialized
uint32_t counter;
counter++;  // Undefined behavior!

// GOOD: Initialized
uint32_t counter = 0;
counter++;  // Now safe
In embedded: Uninitialized variables can contain random RAM values!

Common C Pitfall #2: Integer Overflow
uint8_t count = 255;
count++;  // count = 0 (wraps around!)

// Safer with overflow check:
if (count &lt; UINT8_MAX) {
    count++;
}
Unsigned overflow is defined (wraps), signed overflow is undefined!

Common C Pitfall #3: Pointer Dereferencing NULL
uint32_t *ptr = NULL;
*ptr = 100;  // CRASH! (HardFault on MCU)

// Always check before dereferencing:
if (ptr != NULL) {
    *ptr = 100;
}
On MCU: NULL pointer dereference triggers HardFault exception.

Common C Pitfall #4: Buffer Overrun
uint8_t buffer[10];
buffer[15] = 0xAA;  // Out of bounds! Memory corruption!

// Safe version with bounds check:
uint8_t index = 15;
if (index &lt; sizeof(buffer)) {
    buffer[index] = 0xAA;
}
This is how stack overflows happen!

Common C Pitfall #5: Forgetting & for scanf/pointers
uint32_t value;

// WRONG:
scanf("%d", value);  // Passes value, not address!

// CORRECT:
scanf("%d", &value);  // Passes address of value
In embedded: Similar issue when passing to HAL functions.

