Task (Thread) concept
A task is an independent unit of execution:

Has its own function (entry point)
Has its own stack (local variables)
Has a priority level
Can be in different states

void MyTask(void *argument) {
    // Task initialization

    while (1) {
        // Task work
        // Delay or wait for event
    }
}
Infinite loop is normal - tasks run forever (usually)

Context switching
When scheduler switches tasks:

Save current task’s CPU registers (context)
Save stack pointer to task’s TCB
Select next task to run
Load new task’s context from TCB
Jump to new task’s code

TCB = Task Control Block (data structure for each task)
// Simplified TCB concept
struct TCB {
    void *stack_pointer;
    uint32_t priority;
    char name[16];
    // ... more fields
};

