Delay vs busy-wait
WRONG - Busy wait (CPU spinning):
void BadTask(void *argument) {
    for(;;) {
        toggle_led();
        HAL_Delay(500);  // HAL_Delay is busy-wait!
    }
}
Problem: CPU is 100% busy, no other task can run!
CORRECT - RTOS delay:
void GoodTask(void *argument) {
    for(;;) {
        toggle_led();
        osDelay(500);  // RTOS delay - CPU is free
    }
}

Complete example: Two LED tasks
void FastLEDTask(void *argument) {   // Task 1: own infinite loop
    for(;;) {
        HAL_GPIO_TogglePin(LED1_GPIO_Port, LED1_Pin);
        osDelay(100);                // Sleeps 100ms — other tasks run
    }
}

void SlowLEDTask(void *argument) {   // Task 2: independent loop
    for(;;) {
        HAL_GPIO_TogglePin(LED2_GPIO_Port, LED2_Pin);
        osDelay(500);                // Sleeps 500ms — other tasks run
    }
}

// In main():
osKernelInitialize();                            // Set up kernel internals
osThreadNew(FastLEDTask, NULL, &fastLED_attr);   // Create task 1
osThreadNew(SlowLEDTask, NULL, &slowLED_attr);   // Create task 2
osKernelStart();                                 // Scheduler takes over

Both LEDs blink independently at different rates — no timing conflict. osDelay() puts a task to sleep so the other can run. osThreadNew() registers a task with the scheduler; NULL is passed as *argument. After osKernelStart(), main() never continues.

Scheduler startup
Critical sequence in main():
int main(void) {
    HAL_Init();
    SystemClock_Config();
    MX_GPIO_Init();
    // ... other peripheral init

    MX_FREERTOS_Init();  // Creates tasks

    osKernelStart();     // Start scheduler

    // Scheduler takes over — main() stops here
    while(1) {}
}
After osKernelStart(): Only tasks run, main() is gone

