The shared variable problem


Naïve approach (simple but dangerous):
volatile uint8_t sensor_value = 0;

void SensorTask(void *arg) {
    for(;;) {
        sensor_value = read_adc();  // Write
        osDelay(100);
    }
}

void DisplayTask(void *arg) {
    for(;;) {
        update_display(sensor_value);  // Read
        osDelay(200);
    }
}

What could go wrong?

Data corruption on multi-byte writes
Non-atomic struct updates
Torn reads (old + new fields mixed)
Timing-dependent bugs
Works 999 times, fails on 1000th

Race condition example
Multi-byte data corruption:
volatile uint32_t sensor_data = 0;  // 32-bit value

void SensorTask(void *arg) {
    for(;;) {
        sensor_data = read_32bit_sensor();
        // ARM Cortex-M: single 32-bit write = 1 STR instruction
        // → atomic, no context switch can split it
        osDelay(100);
    }
}
ARM Cortex-M: 32-bit writes are atomic, so this works…
But what about structs?
struct SensorPacket {
    uint32_t timestamp;  // write 1 — STR instruction
    uint16_t value;      // write 2 — STRH instruction
    uint8_t status;      // write 3 — STRB instruction
    // 3 separate writes → context switch can happen
    // between ANY of them → reader sees partial update
};  // NOT atomic!

Queue operations


CMSIS-RTOS2:
uint16_t reading = 1234;
uint16_t received;

// Send
osMessageQueuePut(sensorQueue,
    &reading, 0, osWaitForever);

// Receive
osMessageQueueGet(sensorQueue,
    &received, NULL, osWaitForever);

FreeRTOS native:
uint16_t reading = 1234;
uint16_t received;

// Send
xQueueSend(sensorQueue,
    &reading, portMAX_DELAY);

// Receive
xQueueReceive(sensorQueue,
    &received, portMAX_DELAY);

Blocking behavior


osMessageQueuePut() with timeout:
osStatus_t status = osMessageQueuePut(
    sensorQueue, &reading, 0, timeout);

if (status == osOK) {
    // Data sent successfully
} else if (status == osErrorTimeout) {
    // Queue full, timeout expired
} else if (status == osErrorResource) {
    // Queue full (timeout = 0)
}

Timeout options (4th argument):



Value
Behavior




0
Don’t wait — return osErrorResource if full


osWaitForever
Sleep task until space is available


100
Sleep up to 100 ms, return osErrorTimeout if still full



Same pattern applies to osMessageQueueGet().

Producer-consumer pattern
One task produces data, another consumes it — the queue sits in between. Neither task knows about the other; they only know the queue.
// Producer: reads ADC at 10 Hz
// Put() blocks if queue is full — waits for consumer to free space
void SensorTask(void *arg) {
    uint16_t reading;
    for(;;) {
        reading = HAL_ADC_GetValue(&hadc1);
        osMessageQueuePut(sensorQueue, &reading, 0, osWaitForever);
        osDelay(100);
    }
}

// Consumer: processes data as it arrives
// Get() blocks if queue is empty — waits for producer to add data
void DisplayTask(void *arg) {
    uint16_t value;
    for(;;) {
        osMessageQueueGet(sensorQueue, &value, NULL, osWaitForever);
        update_display(value);
    }
}

Queue with structs
Queues aren’t limited to single values — you can send entire structs. The queue copies sizeof(SensorReading_t) bytes per item, so the receiver gets all fields together atomically.
typedef struct {
    uint32_t timestamp;
    uint16_t adc_value;
    uint8_t  channel;
} SensorReading_t;

// Queue holds 5 structs (item size = 7 bytes each)
osMessageQueueId_t dataQueue;
dataQueue = osMessageQueueNew(5, sizeof(SensorReading_t), NULL);

// Send: all 3 fields go in as one item
SensorReading_t reading = {
    .timestamp = HAL_GetTick(),
    .adc_value = HAL_ADC_GetValue(&hadc1),
    .channel = 0
};
osMessageQueuePut(dataQueue, &reading, 0, osWaitForever);

// Receive: all 3 fields come out together
SensorReading_t received;
osMessageQueueGet(dataQueue, &received, NULL, osWaitForever);

Queue from ISR
Use FromISR variants in interrupt handlers:
void HAL_ADC_ConvCpltCallback(ADC_HandleTypeDef *hadc) {
    uint16_t value = HAL_ADC_GetValue(hadc);

    // Flag: will be set to pdTRUE if sending to the queue
    // wakes a higher-priority task that was blocked on it
    BaseType_t xHigherPriorityTaskWoken = pdFALSE;

    // FromISR version: never blocks, safe to call from interrupt
    // (regular xQueueSend would try to block → crash in ISR)
    xQueueSendFromISR(sensorQueue, &value, &xHigherPriorityTaskWoken);

    // If a higher-priority task was woken, switch to it immediately
    // when the ISR exits (otherwise it waits until next tick → latency)
    portYIELD_FROM_ISR(xHigherPriorityTaskWoken);
}

Never call blocking functions from an ISR! osMessageQueuePut() with a non-zero timeout will crash the system. Use xQueueSendFromISR() (FreeRTOS native) — it is always non-blocking and ISR-safe.

The shared resource problem
Multiple tasks accessing same peripheral:
void Task1(void *arg) {
    for(;;) {
        printf("Task 1 message\n");  // Uses UART
        osDelay(100);
    }
}

void Task2(void *arg) {
    for(;;) {
        printf("Task 2 message\n");  // Uses UART
        osDelay(150);
    }
}
// printf sends one character at a time over UART.
// Same priority → RTOS time-slices (round-robin), so the
// tick can fire mid-printf and switch to the other task.
Result: Task 1 mTask 2 messaessage — characters interleaved!

Creating a mutex


CubeMX (Mutexes tab):



Setting
Value




Mutex Name
UartMutex


Type
Priority Inherit




Or manually in code:
// CMSIS-RTOS2
osMutexId_t uartMutex;
const osMutexAttr_t attr = {
    .name = "UartMutex",
    .attr_bits = osMutexPrioInherit,
};
uartMutex = osMutexNew(&attr);

// FreeRTOS native
SemaphoreHandle_t uartMutex;
uartMutex = xSemaphoreCreateMutex();

The mutex doesn’t know about UART — it’s just a lock. You decide which mutex protects which resource, and every task must follow the same acquire/release convention.

Using a mutex
osMutexId_t uartMutex;

void Task1(void *arg) {
    for(;;) {
        // Acquire mutex (blocks if held by another task)
        osMutexAcquire(uartMutex, osWaitForever);

        // Critical section - safe to use UART
        printf("Task 1 complete message\n");

        // Release mutex
        osMutexRelease(uartMutex);

        osDelay(100);
    }
}
Now output is always complete lines!

Mutex with timeout
osStatus_t status = osMutexAcquire(uartMutex, 100);  // 100ms timeout

if (status == osOK) {
    // Got mutex, safe to proceed
    printf("Protected output\n");
    osMutexRelease(uartMutex);
} else if (status == osErrorTimeout) {
    // Couldn't get mutex in time
    // Handle error or retry
}






Warning


Forgetting to release a mutex causes deadlock. Use a consistent acquire → work → release pattern. Consider wrapping in a helper function.

Deadlock example


// Task 1
osMutexAcquire(mutexA, osWaitForever);
osMutexAcquire(mutexB, osWaitForever);
//            ↑ Blocks here!
// ...
osMutexRelease(mutexB);
osMutexRelease(mutexA);

// Task 2
osMutexAcquire(mutexB, osWaitForever);
osMutexAcquire(mutexA, osWaitForever);
//            ↑ Blocks here!
// ...
osMutexRelease(mutexA);
osMutexRelease(mutexB);

What happens:

Task 1 acquires mutexA — succeeds
RTOS switches to Task 2 → acquires mutexB — succeeds
Task 2 tries mutexA — blocked (Task 1 holds it)
Task 1 tries mutexB — blocked (Task 2 holds it)
Neither can proceed — both wait forever!

Prevention: Always acquire mutexes in the same order (A → B) in every task.

Pattern: Command dispatcher


typedef struct {
    uint8_t command;
    uint8_t param;
} Command_t;
UI sends commands via queue, motor task receives and executes. Tasks are fully decoupled.

// UI task — producer
Command_t cmd = {CMD_START_MOTOR, 50};
osMessageQueuePut(cmdQueue, &cmd, 0, 0);

// Motor task — consumer
Command_t cmd;
osMessageQueueGet(cmdQueue, &cmd,
    NULL, osWaitForever);
execute_command(cmd.command, cmd.param);

Pattern: Protected logging
osMutexId_t logMutex;  // shared by all tasks that want to print

// Thread-safe printf wrapper — any task can call this
// without garbled output, because the mutex ensures
// only one task prints at a time
void log_message(const char *fmt, ...) {
    va_list args;

    // Lock: no other task can print until we release
    osMutexAcquire(logMutex, osWaitForever);

    // va_list lets this function accept any printf-style arguments
    // e.g., log_message("val = %d", 42) works like printf
    va_start(args, fmt);
    vprintf(fmt, args);  // sends formatted string over UART
    va_end(args);

    // Unlock: next waiting task can now print
    osMutexRelease(logMutex);
}

// Any task can safely call — no garbled output:
log_message("Sensor %d: value = %d\n", channel, value);

Pattern: Double buffering
uint16_t buffer_A[100];              // two equally-sized buffers
uint16_t buffer_B[100];
uint16_t *write_buffer = buffer_A;   // producer writes here
uint16_t *read_buffer  = buffer_B;   // consumer reads here
osMutexId_t bufferMutex;

// When producer finishes filling write_buffer and consumer
// finishes reading read_buffer, swap the pointers so they
// switch roles. Mutex protects the swap so no task sees
// a half-updated pointer.
void swap_buffers(void) {
    osMutexAcquire(bufferMutex, osWaitForever);
    uint16_t *temp = write_buffer;   // save current write pointer
    write_buffer = read_buffer;      // producer now writes to old read buffer
    read_buffer = temp;              // consumer now reads what producer just filled
    osMutexRelease(bufferMutex);
}
Producer fills one buffer while consumer reads the other — no waiting, no data corruption

Task design
// SensorTask: Produces raw readings
void SensorTask(void *arg) {
    uint16_t raw;
    for(;;) {
        HAL_ADC_Start(&hadc1);
        HAL_ADC_PollForConversion(&hadc1, 10);
        raw = HAL_ADC_GetValue(&hadc1);
        osMessageQueuePut(rawQueue, &raw, 0, 0);
        osDelay(100);
    }
}

// ProcessTask: Consumes raw, produces averaged
void ProcessTask(void *arg) {
    uint16_t raw, avg;
    uint32_t sum = 0;
    uint8_t count = 0;
    for(;;) {
        osMessageQueueGet(rawQueue, &raw, NULL, osWaitForever);
        sum += raw;
        if (++count &gt;= 4) {
            avg = sum / 4;
            osMessageQueuePut(avgQueue, &avg, 0, 0);
            sum = 0;
            count = 0;
        }
    }
}

I2C bus lockup and recovery
Problem: Slave holds SDA LOW indefinitely





I2C bus lockup comparison


Causes: Noise during transaction, master reset during transfer, slave firmware crash

Recovery procedure:

Toggle SCL 9+ times (clock out stuck byte)
Generate STOP condition
If still stuck: power cycle slave

void I2C_BusRecovery(void) {
    for(int i = 0; i &lt; 9; i++) {
        HAL_GPIO_WritePin(SCL_GPIO, SCL_PIN,
                          GPIO_PIN_RESET);
        HAL_Delay(1);
        HAL_GPIO_WritePin(SCL_GPIO, SCL_PIN,
                          GPIO_PIN_SET);
        HAL_Delay(1);
    }
    // Generate STOP
    HAL_GPIO_WritePin(SDA_GPIO, SDA_PIN,
                      GPIO_PIN_RESET);
    HAL_Delay(1);
    HAL_GPIO_WritePin(SDA_GPIO, SDA_PIN,
                      GPIO_PIN_SET);
}

MPU6050 initialization sequence
#define MPU6050_ADDR  0x68   // 7-bit I2C address (AD0 pin tied LOW)
#define PWR_MGMT_1    0x6B   // Power management register (sleep/clock)
#define GYRO_CONFIG   0x1B   // Gyroscope full-scale range select
#define ACCEL_CONFIG  0x1C   // Accelerometer full-scale range select
#define WHO_AM_I      0x75   // Device-ID register (returns 0x68 on MPU6050)

HAL_StatusTypeDef MPU6050_Init(I2C_HandleTypeDef *hi2c) {
    uint8_t data;                                  // scratch byte for reg I/O

    // 1. Verify device is present by reading WHO_AM_I
    //    HAL shifts address left by 1 to make room for the R/W bit.
    HAL_I2C_Mem_Read(hi2c, MPU6050_ADDR &lt;&lt; 1, WHO_AM_I, 1, &data, 1, 100);
    if (data != 0x68) return HAL_ERROR;            // wrong ID → wiring/addr bug

    // 2. Wake the sensor: clear SLEEP bit (bit 6) and pick a stable clock.
    data = 0x01;                                   // CLKSEL=1 → PLL w/ X-gyro
    HAL_I2C_Mem_Write(hi2c, MPU6050_ADDR &lt;&lt; 1, PWR_MGMT_1, 1, &data, 1, 100);
    HAL_Delay(100);                                // PLL takes ~50 ms to lock

    // 3. Select gyro full-scale: ±250 °/s (most sensitive, 131 LSB/°/s).
    data = 0x00;                                   // FS_SEL=0 in bits 4:3
    HAL_I2C_Mem_Write(hi2c, MPU6050_ADDR &lt;&lt; 1, GYRO_CONFIG, 1, &data, 1, 100);

    // 4. Select accel full-scale: ±2 g (most sensitive, 16384 LSB/g).
    data = 0x00;                                   // AFS_SEL=0 in bits 4:3
    HAL_I2C_Mem_Write(hi2c, MPU6050_ADDR &lt;&lt; 1, ACCEL_CONFIG, 1, &data, 1, 100);
    return HAL_OK;                                 // sensor is ready to read
}

Walk the four-step init sequence live. Step 1 is the WHO_AM_I sanity check — if this fails, there is no point in continuing; the bus is broken or the address is wrong. Step 2 is the mandatory wake-up: the chip powers up in sleep mode, and until you clear the SLEEP bit it ignores every other register. The 100 ms delay is for the PLL to lock — skipping it is the #1 cause of “I read zeros for a second, then real data kicks in”. Steps 3 and 4 pick the most-sensitive ranges. This exact code runs unmodified in the lab.

Reading sensor data
#define ACCEL_XOUT_H  0x3B   // first of 14 sequential data regs (big-endian)

typedef struct {
    int16_t accel_x, accel_y, accel_z;  // raw accel counts (signed 16-bit)
    int16_t temp;                       // raw die temperature
    int16_t gyro_x, gyro_y, gyro_z;     // raw gyro counts (signed 16-bit)
} MPU6050_RawData_t;

HAL_StatusTypeDef MPU6050_ReadRaw(I2C_HandleTypeDef *hi2c,
                                  MPU6050_RawData_t *data) {
    uint8_t buffer[14];                              // 6 accel + 2 temp + 6 gyro

    // Burst read all 14 bytes in one transaction.
    // MPU6050 auto-increments its internal pointer, so a single Mem_Read
    // starting at ACCEL_XOUT_H grabs the whole sample set atomically.
    HAL_StatusTypeDef status = HAL_I2C_Mem_Read(
        hi2c, MPU6050_ADDR &lt;&lt; 1, ACCEL_XOUT_H, 1, buffer, 14, 100);
    if (status != HAL_OK) return status;             // I2C error → abort

    // Combine the high and low bytes. MPU6050 transmits MSB first
    // (big-endian), but STM32 is little-endian, so we must assemble manually.
    data-&gt;accel_x = (int16_t)((buffer[0]  &lt;&lt; 8) | buffer[1]);   // X accel
    data-&gt;accel_y = (int16_t)((buffer[2]  &lt;&lt; 8) | buffer[3]);   // Y accel
    data-&gt;accel_z = (int16_t)((buffer[4]  &lt;&lt; 8) | buffer[5]);   // Z accel
    data-&gt;temp    = (int16_t)((buffer[6]  &lt;&lt; 8) | buffer[7]);   // die temp
    data-&gt;gyro_x  = (int16_t)((buffer[8]  &lt;&lt; 8) | buffer[9]);   // X gyro
    data-&gt;gyro_y  = (int16_t)((buffer[10] &lt;&lt; 8) | buffer[11]);  // Y gyro
    data-&gt;gyro_z  = (int16_t)((buffer[12] &lt;&lt; 8) | buffer[13]);  // Z gyro
    return HAL_OK;
}

Two things to emphasise. First: we do one 14-byte burst read, not seven 2-byte reads. This is faster and — more importantly — guarantees the six motion axes and the temperature are from the same sample. Second: the MPU6050 is big-endian (MSB first), but the STM32 is little-endian, so you can’t just memcpy the buffer into an int16_t array — you must shift the high byte up and OR in the low byte. Students who forget this see data that looks like nonsense, or bytes swapped between axes.

Converting raw data to physical units


Formulas:
\[A_{g} = \frac{A_{raw}}{16384}\]
\[\omega_{dps} = \frac{G_{raw}}{131}\]
\[T_{°C} = \frac{T_{raw}}{340} + 36.53\]

typedef struct {
    float ax, ay, az;  // in g
    float gx, gy, gz;  // in °/s
    float temp;        // in °C
} MPU6050_Data_t;

void MPU6050_Convert(MPU6050_RawData_t *raw,
                     MPU6050_Data_t *data) {
    data-&gt;ax = raw-&gt;accel_x / 16384.0f;
    data-&gt;ay = raw-&gt;accel_y / 16384.0f;
    data-&gt;az = raw-&gt;accel_z / 16384.0f;
    data-&gt;gx = raw-&gt;gyro_x / 131.0f;
    data-&gt;gy = raw-&gt;gyro_y / 131.0f;
    data-&gt;gz = raw-&gt;gyro_z / 131.0f;
    data-&gt;temp = (raw-&gt;temp / 340.0f) + 36.53f;
}


The raw counts are useless by themselves — they need to be divided by the sensitivity constants to become physical units. The constants come from the datasheet and depend on the full-scale range you selected in GYRO_CONFIG/ACCEL_CONFIG. If you change the range, you MUST change these divisors or your units will be off by a factor of 2, 4, or 8. The temperature formula uses a magic offset (36.53) that comes straight out of the datasheet; nobody remembers why those specific numbers — just trust them.

Complete example: main loop
int main(void) {
    HAL_Init();
    SystemClock_Config();
    MX_GPIO_Init();
    MX_I2C1_Init();
    MX_USART2_UART_Init();

    printf("MPU6050 I2C Demo\r\n");
    if (MPU6050_Init(&hi2c1) != HAL_OK) {
        printf("MPU6050 init failed!\r\n");
        Error_Handler();
    }
    printf("MPU6050 initialized\r\n");

    MPU6050_RawData_t raw;
    MPU6050_Data_t data;
    while (1) {
        if (MPU6050_ReadRaw(&hi2c1, &raw) == HAL_OK) {
            MPU6050_Convert(&raw, &data);
            printf("A: %+.2f %+.2f %+.2f g | ", data.ax, data.ay, data.az);
            printf("G: %+.1f %+.1f %+.1f dps | ", data.gx, data.gy, data.gz);
            printf("T: %.1f C\r\n", data.temp);
        } else {
            printf("Read error\r\n");
        }
        HAL_Delay(100);  // 10 Hz
    }
}

