Bit Manipulation for Registers
// SET a bit (turn ON)
GPIOA-&gt;ODR |= (1 &lt;&lt; 5);          // Bit 5 = 1, others unchanged

// CLEAR a bit (turn OFF)
GPIOA-&gt;ODR &= ~(1 &lt;&lt; 5);         // Bit 5 = 0, others unchanged

// TOGGLE a bit
GPIOA-&gt;ODR ^= (1 &lt;&lt; 5);          // Bit 5 flips

// CHECK a bit
if (GPIOA-&gt;IDR & (1 &lt;&lt; 5)) {     // True if bit 5 is set
    // Pin is HIGH
}
Read-Modify-Write: |= and &= read the register, modify one bit, then write back — leaving all other bits unchanged.

GPIO_TypeDef: Registers as a Struct
typedef struct {
    __IO uint32_t MODER;    // offset 0x00 — Mode register
    __IO uint32_t OTYPER;   // offset 0x04 — Output type
    __IO uint32_t OSPEEDR;  // offset 0x08 — Output speed
    __IO uint32_t PUPDR;    // offset 0x0C — Pull-up/pull-down
    __IO uint32_t IDR;      // offset 0x10 — Input data (read-only)
    __IO uint32_t ODR;      // offset 0x14 — Output data
    // ... BSRR, LCKR, AFR[2]
} GPIO_TypeDef;

#define GPIOA ((GPIO_TypeDef *) 0x40020000)
GPIOA-&gt;ODR = read 32-bit value at address 0x40020000 + 0x14 = 0x40020014
This is exactly what the datasheet register map shows!

HAL Callback Pattern
HAL uses weak callbacks — you override them with your own implementation:
// Defined in HAL as __weak (does nothing by default)
void HAL_TIM_PeriodElapsedCallback(TIM_HandleTypeDef *htim) {
    if (htim-&gt;Instance == TIM2) {
        tick_count++;    // Your code here
    }
}
How it works:

Hardware interrupt fires → TIM2_IRQHandler() (in stm32f4xx_it.c)
HAL handler called → HAL_TIM_IRQHandler(&htim2)
HAL clears flags, identifies event, calls → HAL_TIM_PeriodElapsedCallback()
Your code runs inside the callback

Always check htim-&gt;Instance — all timers share the same callback!

