ADC configuration in code
// Enable ADC1 clock
RCC-&gt;APB2ENR |= RCC_APB2ENR_ADC1EN;

// Configure PA0 as analog input
GPIOA-&gt;MODER |= GPIO_MODER_MODE0;    // 0b11 = analog mode

// ADC settings: 12-bit, single conversion
ADC1-&gt;CR1  = 0;
ADC1-&gt;CR2  = ADC_CR2_ADON;
ADC1-&gt;SQR3 = 0;                      // Channel 0 (PA0)

// Sampling time: 480 cycles → R_AIN max ≈ 200 kΩ
ADC1-&gt;SMPR2 = (7U &lt;&lt; 0);             // 0b111 = 480 cycles
The sampling time register is where theory meets hardware.
Change 7U to 4U for 84 cycles (~50 kΩ), or 1U for 15 cycles (~10 kΩ).

Clock setup: The ADC clock comes from APB2 (PCLK2). At 84 MHz PCLK2 with /4 prescaler we get f_ADC = 21 MHz. The max allowed f_ADC for STM32F401 is 36 MHz (DS10086 Table 74), so /2 (42 MHz) would be out of spec — always use /4 or higher.
Register walk-through:

RCC-&gt;APB2ENR: ADC1 sits on the APB2 bus, so we enable its clock here. Without this line every ADC register read returns zero.
GPIOA-&gt;MODER = 0b11: Analog mode disconnects the digital input buffer — reduces leakage and noise. This is also why FT tolerance is lost in analog mode (the upper protection diode is disabled).
ADC1-&gt;CR1 = 0: Clears scan mode, interrupt enables, and resolution bits. 0 means 12-bit, single channel, no interrupt.
ADC1-&gt;CR2 = ADON: Powers up the analog circuitry. The ADC draws extra current when on — in battery applications you would turn it off between conversions.
ADC1-&gt;SQR3 = 0: The “sequence” register. Channel 0 = PA0. If you wanted PA1 you’d write 1 here.
ADC1-&gt;SMPR2: This is the key register from our theory discussion. Each channel gets a 3-bit field selecting sampling cycles. 7 = 480 cycles = longest hold time = highest allowed R_AIN. If your source impedance is low (e.g., op-amp output, &lt; 1 kΩ), you can use fewer cycles for faster throughput.

Why 480 cycles? It’s the safe default — works with almost any source impedance up to ~200 kΩ. The tradeoff is speed: at 21 MHz, one sample takes 480/21M ≈ 23 µs. For a 10 Hz sensor that’s fine; for audio-rate sampling you’d need fewer cycles.

Reading ADC with voltage divider correction
// Start conversion and wait
ADC1-&gt;CR2 |= ADC_CR2_SWSTART;
while (!(ADC1-&gt;SR & ADC_SR_EOC));
uint16_t raw = (uint16_t)ADC1-&gt;DR;

// Convert to voltage at ADC pin (mV)
uint32_t v_adc_mv = (uint32_t)raw * 3300 / 4096;

// Undo divider: V_in = V_adc × (R1 + R2) / R2
#define R1  1000   // 1kΩ
#define R2  2000   // 2kΩ
uint32_t v_input_mv = v_adc_mv * (R1 + R2) / R2;
Example: ADC reads 2482 → v_adc = 2000 mV → v_input = 3000 mV (actual 3V input scaled to 2V by divider)

Conversion formula: raw * 3300 / 4096 converts the 12-bit ADC count (0–4095) to millivolts at the ADC pin, assuming V_REF = 3.3V. We use millivolts (integer math) to avoid floating point — important on Cortex-M4 without FPU enabled.
Divider correction: The ADC only sees the divided-down voltage. To recover the actual input voltage we multiply by (R1 + R2) / R2 — the inverse of the divider ratio. This is done in integer arithmetic so order matters: multiply first, then divide, to avoid truncation to zero.
Watch for overflow: raw * 3300 can reach 4095 × 3300 ≈ 13.5M, which fits in uint32_t (max ~4.3G). But if you chain more multiplications, check that intermediates don’t overflow.

