This works, but…

Every task runs sequentially
Slow tasks block fast tasks
Hard to add new features
Timing depends on loop length
No priority control

Question: What if read_sensors() takes 50ms but a button press needs &lt; 10ms response?
This is where RTOS comes in.

Bare-metal vs FreeRTOS — side by side


Bare-metal (super loop):
int main(void) {
  HAL_Init();
  SystemClock_Config();
  MX_GPIO_Init();
  MX_USART2_UART_Init();
  MX_TIM3_Init();

  while (1) {
    read_sensors();     // 50ms
    update_display();   // 30ms
    check_buttons();    // 5ms
    control_motor();    // 10ms
  }
}
// No priorities, no preemption
// Button response: up to 90ms

FreeRTOS (multi-task):
int main(void) {
  HAL_Init();
  SystemClock_Config();
  MX_GPIO_Init();
  MX_USART2_UART_Init();
  MX_TIM3_Init();
  MX_FREERTOS_Init(); // tasks
  osKernelStart();    // go!
}

void SensorTask(void *arg) {
  for(;;) { read_sensors();
             osDelay(50); }
}
void ButtonTask(void *arg) {
  for(;;) { check_buttons();
             osDelay(10); }
}
// Button response: &lt;10ms

Mental model: multiple super loops


Bare metal — one super loop:
while (1) {
    sensors();    // 50ms
    display();    // 30ms
    buttons();    //  5ms
    motor();      // 10ms
    uart();       // 20ms
}
// Everything sequential,
// everything waits

RTOS — each task is its own loop:
void sensor_task(void *arg) {
    while (1) { sensors(); osDelay(100); }
}
void button_task(void *arg) {
    while (1) { buttons(); osDelay(10); }
}
void motor_task(void *arg) {
    while (1) { motor();   osDelay(10); }
}

Still one CPU — only one task runs at any instant. But the scheduler switches between them, so each task feels like it has the CPU to itself. Button task doesn’t wait for sensors to finish.

Volatile — the #1 embedded bug
// BUG: compiler caches data_ready in register, never re-reads
uint8_t data_ready = 0;

// FIX: forces compiler to read from memory every time
volatile uint8_t data_ready = 0;
Rule: Any variable shared between ISR and main loop must be volatile.

Without volatile, the compiler optimizes the while(!data_ready) loop into an infinite loop because it assumes no one else changes the variable.

ISR best practices

Keep ISRs short — set a flag, get out
Never call HAL_Delay() inside an ISR
Use volatile for shared variables
Clear the interrupt flag (HAL does this for you in callbacks)

volatile uint8_t flag = 0;

void HAL_TIM_PeriodElapsedCallback(TIM_HandleTypeDef *htim) {
    flag = 1;  // Fast — just set flag
}

while (1) {
    if (flag) { process(); flag = 0; }  // Main loop does the work
}

ADC fundamentals
12-bit ADC, VREF = 3.3V:
\[LSB = \frac{3.3}{4096} \approx 0.806\,\text{mV}\]
\[V_{in} = \frac{ADC_{raw}}{4095} \times 3.3\]
Watch out for integer division:
// BUG: integer / integer = 0 for any adc_raw &lt; 4095
voltage = (adc_raw / 4095) * 3.3f;

// FIX: force float division
voltage = (adc_raw / 4095.0f) * 3.3f;

Moving average filter
#define FILTER_SIZE 8
uint16_t buffer[FILTER_SIZE] = {0};
uint32_t sum = 0;
uint8_t index = 0;

uint16_t filter(uint16_t sample) {
    sum -= buffer[index];
    buffer[index] = sample;
    sum += sample;
    index = (index + 1) % FILTER_SIZE;
    return sum / FILTER_SIZE;
}

Circular buffer — O(1) per update. Larger window = smoother but slower response. 8 samples at 10 Hz = 0.8s for full response.

Super loop vs RTOS


Super loop problem:
while (1) {
    sensors();    // 50ms
    display();    // 30ms
    buttons();    //  5ms
    motor();      // 10ms
}
// Button response: up to 95ms!

RTOS solution:
void sensor_task(void *arg) {
    for(;;) { sensors(); osDelay(100); }
}
void button_task(void *arg) {
    for(;;) { buttons(); osDelay(10); }
}
// Button response: ~10ms
// regardless of sensor timing

ADC — Single channel read
// CubeMX: PA0 → ADC1_IN0, 12-bit, 84 cycle sampling

uint16_t read_adc(void) {
    HAL_ADC_Start(&hadc1);                    // Trigger conversion
    HAL_ADC_PollForConversion(&hadc1, 10);    // Wait until done (10ms timeout)
    return HAL_ADC_GetValue(&hadc1);           // Read 12-bit result (0–4095)
}

// Usage:
uint16_t raw = read_adc();
float voltage = (raw / 4095.0f) * 3.3f;       // Don't forget .0f!

Three steps: Start → Poll → GetValue. Forgetting Poll reads stale data. Integer division (raw / 4095 without .0f) always gives 0.

ADC — Multi-channel with runtime switching
// CubeMX: PA0 → ADC1_IN0, PA1 → ADC1_IN1

uint16_t read_adc_channel(uint8_t channel) {
    ADC_ChannelConfTypeDef sConfig = {0};
    sConfig.Channel = (channel == 0) ? ADC_CHANNEL_0 : ADC_CHANNEL_1;
    sConfig.Rank = 1;                          // Always rank 1
    sConfig.SamplingTime = ADC_SAMPLETIME_84CYCLES;

    HAL_ADC_ConfigChannel(&hadc1, &sConfig);   // Load channel into sequencer
    HAL_ADC_Start(&hadc1);
    HAL_ADC_PollForConversion(&hadc1, 10);
    return HAL_ADC_GetValue(&hadc1);
}

// Usage:
uint16_t temp  = read_adc_channel(0);   // PA0
uint16_t light = read_adc_channel(1);   // PA1

ConfigChannel overwrites which channel is in Rank 1 before each conversion. This is manual single-shot — scan mode in CubeMX isn’t required for this approach, but both pins must be configured as ADC inputs.

UART — Transmit with sprintf
// CubeMX: USART2, 115200 baud, Asynchronous
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;

char uart_buf[64];

// Send formatted text:
sprintf(uart_buf, "ADC=%d, V=%.3f\r\n", raw, voltage);
HAL_UART_Transmit(&huart2, (uint8_t *)uart_buf,
                  strlen(uart_buf), HAL_MAX_DELAY);

sprintf formats into a buffer. strlen gives the actual string length (not sizeof which gives the full array size). (uint8_t *) cast is required because HAL expects a byte pointer. \r\n is carriage return + line feed for terminal display.

Timer interrupt — Periodic callback
// CubeMX: TIM2, Prescaler & Period set for desired frequency
// Enable "TIM2 global interrupt" in NVIC settings

// Start timer interrupt (call once in main, before while loop):
HAL_TIM_Base_Start_IT(&htim2);

// Callback — called automatically every timer period:
void HAL_TIM_PeriodElapsedCallback(TIM_HandleTypeDef *htim) {
    if (htim-&gt;Instance == TIM2) {
        HAL_GPIO_TogglePin(GPIOA, GPIO_PIN_5);  // Toggle LED
    }
}

_Start_IT enables the interrupt. The callback fires automatically — you don’t call it. Check htim-&gt;Instance if you have multiple timers. Keep the callback short.

PWM output
// CubeMX: TIM3 CH1 → PA6, PWM Generation CH1
// Prescaler and Period set for desired PWM frequency
// e.g., 84MHz / (83+1) / (999+1) = 1 kHz PWM

// Start PWM (call once in main):
HAL_TIM_PWM_Start(&htim3, TIM_CHANNEL_1);

// Change duty cycle at runtime:
__HAL_TIM_SET_COMPARE(&htim3, TIM_CHANNEL_1, 500);  // 500/1000 = 50%
__HAL_TIM_SET_COMPARE(&htim3, TIM_CHANNEL_1, 250);  // 250/1000 = 25%
__HAL_TIM_SET_COMPARE(&htim3, TIM_CHANNEL_1, 0);    // 0% — off

Duty cycle = Compare value / (ARR + 1). If ARR = 999, then compare = 500 gives 50%. PWM_Start only needs to be called once — after that, just change the compare value to adjust speed.

GPIO interrupt — Button with EXTI
// CubeMX: PC13 (user button) → GPIO_EXTI13, falling edge trigger
// Enable "EXTI line[15:10] interrupts" in NVIC

volatile uint8_t button_pressed = 0;

// Callback — fires on button press:
void HAL_GPIO_EXTI_Callback(uint16_t GPIO_Pin) {
    if (GPIO_Pin == GPIO_PIN_13) {
        button_pressed = 1;
    }
}

// Main loop handles the event:
while (1) {
    if (button_pressed) {
        do_something();
        button_pressed = 0;
    }
}

volatile is mandatory — the variable is modified in ISR, read in main loop. The callback sets a flag and returns immediately. Main loop does the actual work. No debouncing shown here — add HAL_Delay(200) after handling if needed.

Putting it all together — typical main.c
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;

volatile uint8_t timer_flag = 0;
char uart_buf[64];

void HAL_TIM_PeriodElapsedCallback(TIM_HandleTypeDef *htim) {
    if (htim-&gt;Instance == TIM2) timer_flag = 1;
}

int main(void) {
    HAL_Init();
    SystemClock_Config();
    MX_GPIO_Init();
    MX_ADC1_Init();
    MX_USART2_UART_Init();
    MX_TIM2_Init();
    MX_TIM3_Init();

    HAL_TIM_Base_Start_IT(&htim2);             // Start periodic interrupt
    HAL_TIM_PWM_Start(&htim3, TIM_CHANNEL_1);  // Start PWM output

    while (1) {
        if (timer_flag) {
            timer_flag = 0;
            uint16_t adc = read_adc();
            float v = (adc / 4095.0f) * 3.3f;
            __HAL_TIM_SET_COMPARE(&htim3, TIM_CHANNEL_1, adc / 4);  // ADC controls PWM
            sprintf(uart_buf, "V=%.2f duty=%d%%\r\n", v, adc * 100 / 4095);
            HAL_UART_Transmit(&huart2, (uint8_t *)uart_buf, strlen(uart_buf), HAL_MAX_DELAY);
        }
    }
}

Scheduling & preemption recap


Preemptive scheduling:

RTOS checks priorities every tick (1 ms)
Higher-priority Ready task preempts immediately
Same priority → round-robin time-slicing

A task runs until it blocks, gets preempted, or its time slice expires.

What we built last week:
