This is imported and adapted from the EE5203 Week 13 TFT section. It is intentionally a concept slide, not a complete driver. The important ordering is command mode, parameter data, RAM-write command, pixel data via DMA.

HD44780: driver code matches the flow


#define LCD_ADDR (0x27 &lt;&lt; 1)
#define LCD_RS 0x01
#define LCD_E  0x04
#define LCD_BL 0x08

static void lcd_i2c_write(uint8_t v) {
    v |= LCD_BL;  // keep backlight on
    HAL_I2C_Master_Transmit(&hi2c1, LCD_ADDR, &v, 1, 10);
}

static void lcd_pulse(uint8_t v) {
    lcd_i2c_write(v | LCD_E);   // E high
    lcd_i2c_write(v & ~LCD_E);  // falling edge latches nibble
}

static void lcd_write4(uint8_t nibble, bool is_data) {
    uint8_t v = (nibble &lt;&lt; 4) | (is_data ? LCD_RS : 0);
    lcd_pulse(v);
}

void lcd_send(uint8_t byte, bool is_data) {
    lcd_write4(byte &gt;&gt; 4, is_data);         // upper nibble first
    lcd_write4(byte & 0x0F, is_data);       // lower nibble second
    HAL_Delay(is_data ? 1 : 2);             // wait for LCD execution
}

void lcd_putc(char c) { lcd_send((uint8_t)c, true); }
void lcd_puts(const char *s) { while (*s) lcd_putc(*s++); }

Connect code to the flow

lcd_puts() = print string
lcd_putc() = print one character
lcd_send() = send byte
first lcd_write4() = upper nibble to PCF8574
second lcd_write4() = lower nibble to PCF8574
lcd_pulse() = E high then low over I2C
final delay = LCD executes internally

Backpack mapping: common modules use P0=RS, P2=E, P3=backlight, P4-P7=D4-D7.


This is not a complete reusable driver; it is the smallest code path that proves the I2C-backpack flow. Mention that production code should return status codes and handle I2C timeouts explicitly.

Bitmap-to-pixel code: one character


void ST7789_WriteChar(uint16_t x, uint16_t y, char ch,
                      FontDef font, uint16_t fg, uint16_t bg) {
    uint32_t char_offset = (ch - 32) * font.height;

    SetWindow(x, y, x + font.width - 1, y + font.height - 1);
    DC_HIGH();                       // following bytes are pixel data

    for (uint32_t row = 0; row &lt; font.height; row++) {
        uint16_t bits = font.data[char_offset + row];

        for (uint32_t col = 0; col &lt; font.width; col++) {
            uint16_t color =
                ((bits &lt;&lt; col) & 0x8000) ? fg : bg;

            uint8_t px[2] = { color &gt;&gt; 8, color & 0xFF };
            HAL_SPI_Transmit(&hspi1, px, 2, HAL_MAX_DELAY);
        }
    }
}

Connect code to bitmap

char_offset selects the character
row selects one bitmap row
col tests one bit in that row
bit = 1 -&gt; foreground RGB565
bit = 0 -&gt; background RGB565
each pixel becomes two SPI bytes

Why it works: the ST7789 auto-increments inside the active address window.


This is adapted from the EE5203 Week 13 lab driver. The code intentionally uses blocking SPI because a single character is small. For large rectangles or images, use DMA chunks.

Driver internals: rectangle fill with DMA chunks


void ST7789_FillRect(uint16_t x, uint16_t y,
                     uint16_t w, uint16_t h, uint16_t color) {
    SetWindow(x, y, x + w - 1, y + h - 1);

    static uint8_t buf[512];         // 256 RGB565 pixels
    uint8_t hi = color &gt;&gt; 8;
    uint8_t lo = color & 0xFF;

    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 256; i++) {
        buf[2*i]     = hi;           // RGB565 high byte
        buf[2*i + 1] = lo;           // RGB565 low byte
    }

    uint32_t remaining = (uint32_t)w * h;
    DC_HIGH();                       // following bytes are pixels

    while (remaining &gt; 0) {
        uint16_t n = (remaining &gt; 256) ? 256 : remaining;
        spi_tx_done = 0;
        HAL_SPI_Transmit_DMA(&hspi1, buf, n * 2);
        while (!spi_tx_done) { }     // simple, but CPU blocks here
        remaining -= n;
    }
}

Why chunking matters

Full frame: 115,200 bytes
STM32F401RE SRAM: 96 KB
A full framebuffer does not fit comfortably
A 512-byte chunk is reusable
DMA moves the chunk to SPI
Callback sets spi_tx_done = 1

Design tradeoff: simple chunking saves RAM, but the wait loop still blocks the CPU.


This slide answers why the driver does not allocate a full 240 x 240 framebuffer. The library repeats a small RGB565 buffer until the rectangle is filled. This is a useful embedded design pattern: small reusable buffers instead of large framebuffers.

Font data structure: font descriptor + row table


typedef struct {
    const uint8_t width;       // pixels per character row
    const uint8_t height;      // rows per character
    const uint16_t *data;      // all character rows
} FontDef;

static const uint16_t Font7x10_Table[] = {
    // ASCII 32: ' ' -&gt; 10 rows
    0x0000, 0x0000, /* ... */

    // ASCII 33: '!' -&gt; next 10 rows
    0x0C00, 0x0C00, /* ... */
};

FontDef Font_7x10 = {
    7,                 // width
    10,                // height
    Font7x10_Table     // pointer to the row table
};

What is stored where?

FontDef describes the whole font
One character is not a separate struct
Characters are stored sequentially in Font7x10_Table
For Font_7x10, each character uses 10 uint16_t rows
ASCII space starts at index 0
ASCII ! starts after 10 rows

Offset formula
char_offset = (ch - 32) * font.height;


Emphasize the memory layout: one descriptor points to one flat row table. For fixed-size fonts, finding a character is just pointer arithmetic. This also reinforces why font.height is used in the offset calculation.

